<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11821"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 19, 2005 - 12:42 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->People, you are not going to believe this - this is WAY COOL!  We were right about this symbol ‚Äì everything.  Holy cow!  Somebody wake up Johno.  &#40;Embersage, you never sleep anyway!&#41;  Check this out...... <BR> <BR>It&#39;s a friggin&#39; ZODIAC symbol! <BR> <BR>Turn on Unicode encoding in your browser and follow along.... <BR> <BR>I was having lunch today with my Japanese langauage contact &#40;who gave me a translation of a cold case in Japan for Verm_inve&#41;.  I showed her the BTK symbol, drawn in the usual way, but without eyes &#40;meaning turned with the B facing down, like glasses or breasts, like we&#39;ve all been drawing it all along&#41;.  She said there are no kanji with that curved part of the B, which makes sense because of the straight strokes used for Chinese characters &#40;i.e., the Japanese kanji&#41;, except in calligraphy. <BR> <BR>Anyway, then I said ‚Äúhow about if the ‚ÄúB‚Äù part was squared off, like a horizonal domino, meaning two squares side-by-side, sitting on top of the ‚ÄúT-K‚Äù part &#40;‚Äúki‚Äù, Êú®&#41;?  Like a digital ‚Äú8‚Äù on a cheap LED calculator &#40;2 squares joined&#41;, or like the rising sun symbol ‚Äúnichi‚Äù &#40;Êó•&#41; on its side, and then on top of the &#34;T-K&#34;.  She said that the two-square character is generally vertical when used in kanji, although it is occasionally seen horizonal &#40;more often the analogous three-square character is seen horizontal&#41;, but never in combination with the ‚Äúki‚Äù character.  Put more simply, she said that even squaring off the ‚ÄúB‚Äù in the BTK symbol, it wasn&#39;t a real kanji.  The closest thing is the kanji for ‚Äúchestnut‚Äù &#40;ritsu, Ê†ó&#41;, which has a 3-square with an extra handle on top, resting on top of ‚Äúki‚Äù.  To wit, a 3-eyed BTK symbol with alien granny glasses.  Negative. <BR> <BR>Then I drew her the character that Johno pointed out on the web page, that he thought looked like the BTK symbol &#40;Âë≥&#41;.  That character in Japanese is called ‚Äúmi‚Äù, and means ‚Äútaste‚Äù, and consists of two parts.  The elongated square on the left generally means ‚Äúmouth‚Äù &#40;‚Äúko‚Äù, Âè£, usually more squared when alone, more compressed when combined&#41;, and the BTK-symbol-looking thing &#40;Êú™&#41;, which is like ‚Äúki‚Äù with an extra bar on top, is also called ‚Äúmi‚Äù &#40;go figure&#41;, and is used to mean ‚Äúfuture‚Äù, ‚Äústill‚Äù, or ‚Äúnot yet‚Äù in combination with other symbols. <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s the cool part.  She said &#40;nearly verbatim&#41;, ‚ÄúWe never use that part by itself in Japanese regular writing.  It only used in Japanese Zodiac.  It used for &#39;year of sheep&#39;, or &#39;year of lamb&#39; or &#39;year of ram&#39; &#40;Êú™Âπ¥, hitsujidoshi&#41;, not regular Japanese kanji for sheep.‚Äù  I asked her 3 times just to make absolutely sure what she was saying, and then I wrote it down.   Zodiac.  Zodiac.  Zodiac. <BR> <BR>Sure enough, if you go out on the web, you will find that there are two different characters used for the Year of the Sheep in the Chinese and Japanese Zodiacs.  The common word used for the animal in normal conversation is the one that looks like it has horns &#40;Áæä&#41;, but the more traditional one used in the Asian Zodiac is basically the BTK symbol without the curve of the B or the dots for eyes &#40;Êú™&#41;.  It&#39;s called ‚Äúhitsuji‚Äù in Japanese, and the year of the sheep is called ‚Äúhitsujidoshi‚Äù &#40;Êú™Âπ¥, sheep year&#41;. <BR> <BR>The Years of the Sheep are:  1907, 1919, 1931, 1943, 1955, 1967, 1979, 1991, 2003.  You guys are the Zodiac and BTK timeline experts ‚Äì you figure it out.  I&#39;m just a language and code guy.  Maybe you can make sense of why he chose it, other than he could fit his name into it. <BR> <BR>Here are some web sites.  Check &#39;em out: <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.takase.com/Zodiac/Astrology.htm" target=_top>http://www.takase.com/Zodiac/Astrology.htm</a> <BR> <BR><a href="http://asnic.utexas.edu/asnic/countries/china/czod.html" target=_top>http://asnic.utexas.edu/asnic/countries/china/czod .html</a> <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.onmarkproductions.com/html/8-zodiac-protectors.shtml" target=_top>http://www.onmarkproductions.com/html/8-zodiac-pro tectors.shtml</a> <BR> <BR>It all makes sense.  You can morph ‚Äúhitsuji‚Äù into the BTK symbol by merely drawing the curves of the B on it, and touching up the top part of the center line a bit with an eraser, so it&#39;s not too obvious what it is.  In reality, he spaced the lines a little extra-wide to make the B scale properly, and to accent the boobs, but that&#39;s pretty much it.  All along I was thinking it was just some dopey junior-high doodle, when in reality it was a surprisingly sophisticated Zodiac ‚Äúhomage‚Äù symbol.  The boobs are really far less of a message than a diversion or a camouflage.  I have a secret symbol for my initials, which a few people know and which they generally think is pretty clever.  I would say that BTK&#39;s is just as thoughtful. <BR> <BR>All this time I thought that you Zodiac/BTK linkers were pushing the margins a little hard.  Heck, now we know.  BTK was a total Zodiac fan-boy.  You guys had him pegged from go.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
