<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12537"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 3:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->MAIL FROM CURIOUS ABOUT THE SYMBOL <BR> <BR>While Rader may not read Mandarin but may have been familiar with Japanese, many Japanese `kanji&#39; characters are derived from Mandarin. The threads about the lamb symbol is very interesting because on the Chinese zodiac, the lamb &#40;or sheep&#41; is the symbol of the peacemaker. Which is quite different from the western Judeo-Christian mythology - lamb to slaughter, sacrifice, the evil goat,  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
